From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 10:41:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Add a 32-bit build for Apple's ARMv7 chips

make M1N1_ARCH=arm builds m1n1-armv7.macho for the A5, A5X, A6 and A6X.
The 32-bit iBoot starts it as its kernel, with XNU's boot arguments and
the Apple device tree, so one binary serves every board as on the 64-bit
chips; nothing in it is board specific.

The processor layer is new, in src/arm32/: entry and self-relocation,
exception vectors with the proxy's guards, an identity-mapped MMU of
1 MiB sections (ARMv7 has no data cache without it), time from the
interrupt controller's 24 MHz timebase (the cores have no architected
timer), the version 1 watchdog, and the Linux hand-off: the board tree is
filled with memory, framebuffer, serial number, addresses and seed from
the Apple device tree, initrd and tree go above the 128 MiB the zImage
decompressor claims, and the zImage is entered with r0 = 0, r1 = ~0,
r2 = tree, MMU and caches off.

USB is the same DWC2 controller as on A7-A10 behind the S5L PHY, which
iBoot powers down before it starts a kernel: it is started the way the
chip's own firmware does. These chips have no cache-coherent DMA, so the
controller's transfer buffers come from one uncached section.

The shared code is used as it is: console, framebuffer, proxy, payloads
and the boot manager. It keeps addresses in u64; the few places that
depend on the processor are split by __arm__, and drivers for hardware
these chips do not have are left out of the build. u64 is unsigned long
long here, so m1n1's printf reads %l as 64 bits on this build, which
keeps every "%lx with a u64" caller right. The Rust core is built with
the image's relocation model, as m1n1 is position independent and the
target's prebuilt core is not.

Main storage answers "not supported" until the next commit. The 64-bit
build is unchanged: its C code compiles to the same instructions (line
numbers in assertions aside).

Run in QEMU (Cortex-A9, a synthetic Apple device tree): start-up, MMU,
framebuffer console, proxy over the UART with orchard's client including
recovery from a guarded bad read, and a payload boot into a stand-in
zImage that got the expected registers and a filled tree. Not run on
hardware.

Assisted-by: LLM
